# husk.rc — commands husk runs at startup, exactly as if you had typed them.
#
# The name is the oldest one in the system: "rc" is runcom, "run commands",
# from CTSS in the early 1960s. Louis Pouzin wrote it, and coined the word
# "shell" for the same idea — a decade before Unix had either.
#
# This file is the BUILD's copy: it lives at etc/husk.rc in the source tree
# and is written onto the FAT partition as /fat/husk.rc on every image build.
# You can edit /fat/husk.rc from inside the OS and it will take effect on the
# next boot — but the next `make` puts this version back, so anything you want
# to keep belongs here in the repo.
#
# Lines starting with # are comments. Blank lines are ignored. Everything else
# is a husk command line with the full shell available: pipelines, redirection,
# background (&), $?.

# ── the logging daemon ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Uncommented, this hands logging off from the in-kernel logd to the ring-3
# one, which appends to a file instead of pumping every byte out the serial
# port. Measured on a DEBUG_SCHEDULER|DEBUG_DETAILED boot: ~60-100% of a core
# in the kernel, ~20% here — the serial port was the expense, not the daemon.
# Left commented so a plain build boots exactly as it always has — and NOTE:
# the default boot entry already launches logd via the LOGD= cmdline flag
# (earlier than any rc can, so the file gets the whole boot). The log claim
# is exclusive: enable this only on a boot entry WITHOUT LOGD=, or the
# second daemon will announce the conflict and exit.
#logd /fat/os64.log &

# ── personal config ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The machine speaks GMT (kernel default since 2026-08-04); a timezone
# belongs to the human, so it lives here. Classic V7 TZ format — offset
# counts hours WEST of UTC (their little prank), and the DST rules ride
# along; libos64's calendar honors both. The clock is the corner widget
# (Chris's app) — started here, it inherits your TZ through the environment
# like every other child of the rc.
#export TZ=EST5EDT
#clock &

# ── the prompt ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# $PROMPT sets it; unset, husk prompts "husk> " the way it always has. The
# escapes:
#
#   \w  working directory          \?  the last command's exit status
#   \W  its last component         \j  background jobs being tracked
#   \t  time, HH:MM:SS             \n  newline, for a two-line prompt
#   \d  date, YYYY-MM-DD           \\  a literal backslash
#
# The string is expanded ONCE, here, like any other command line — so
# "$HOSTNAME:\w" works and picks up whatever the line above set, and that is
# why there is no \h escape. What husk does NOT do is re-expand the prompt on
# every print, which is bash's most famous foot-gun: the escapes above are a
# closed vocabulary that cannot turn into syntax. An escape it does not know is
# printed verbatim, so a typo shows up in your prompt instead of vanishing.
#
# WHICH MAKES $? AND \? DIFFERENT, and it is the one mistake worth warning
# about: "$?" is expanded HERE and freezes the status that was current when
# this file ran — a literal 0, for the rest of the session — while "\?" is
# evaluated at every prompt. The frozen one looks alive, because a ticking \t
# next to it updates every second while the status never moves. $NAME for what
# does not change, an escape for what does.
#
# Two the shipped default deliberately lacks, because os64 has nothing to put
# in them: \u (no users) and \$'s #-for-root (no privilege levels).
#
#export PROMPT="\w $ "
#export PROMPT="[\t] \W \?> "
